Minato's patch to frameworks/base's core/java/android/view/TextureView.java at android-15.0.0_r1 (Apache-2.0),
ADR-0058 d): a TextureView in a window drawn in software shows its SurfaceTexture's frames, as Android shows them in
a hardware accelerated window.

Why: Minato's windows draw in software (ADR-0043 decision 2), and AOSP's TextureView draws only on a hardware canvas,
through a TextureLayer of hwui's render thread: in software it never makes its SurfaceTexture, its listener never
hears onSurfaceTextureAvailable, and it draws nothing. An app's window on Android is hardware accelerated, so apps
that show video, a camera preview or GL through a TextureView would show nothing on Minato.

What: on the canvas of the window it is attached to (ViewRootImpl.drawsInSoftware), the view makes its SurfaceTexture
and calls its listener as getTextureLayer does, and draws its current frame: TextureView's natives
(android_view_TextureView.cpp, ours) copy the newest queued frame into a Bitmap on the CPU (nMinatoLatch), where hwui
binds it to a texture, a named mechanism difference (a copy per frame), drawn with the SurfaceTexture's transform, the
view's (setTransform) and the layer's paint, filtered. There the view is its own layer (getLayerType answers NONE
while the window draws it), since View's draw would put a hardware layer through its drawing cache, a Bitmap canvas.
getBitmap draws the frame with the SurfaceTexture's transform alone, as hwui's readback does (texture-view-test).
Any other software canvas, a drawing cache or an app's own, shows the view nothing, as on Android.

